TEST SUITE -- VACUITY FINDINGS, PARTIALLY ADDRESSED
Full detail: private/project_planning/test_vacuity_audit_20260912.md (V1-V17, 23/~25 files
covered) and vacuity_audit_2026-09-12.md (S1-S14, diff scope). Current anti-vacuity
regressions cover production-format refusal, sign partitions, scheduler gates, and
no-vdG/perception-loss paths. The remaining historical audit items are not a production
build gate unless their corresponding behavior is exercised or changed again. 

CURRENT-FORMAT REBUILD STATUS (no current-format library exists on disk yet, DR-61;
the sign-partition implementation is in the current readers, writers, and tests;
a clean production rebuild fleet was submitted 2026-09-21, DR-63, IN PROGRESS
(MIN_SUPPORT=500, 1,529 jobs, ids 5039771-5041299); parent DB is
/wynton/group/degradolab/skt/docking/databases/prepwizard_BioLiP2_repaired, identity
0119d9ab2502371f..., every later step must point at it)
- `--record-min-support` is the single lever on both quadratic protonation-pooling steps
  (the enumerator's and `make_sge_scripts_for_frags`' `prepare_fragments`). 48% of keys have
  support 1 and can never clear any sane threshold. Writing the dict at
  `--record-min-support 2` is free and 25 would cut the dict 7x, but it costs the ability to
  answer frontier questions below that floor and makes a lower top-up a rebuild. Not worth it
  once the census fit in an hour; revisit only if pooling becomes the bottleneck. Cost model,
  measured: pooling is BILINEAR in (charged keys x total keys), not quadratic in charged
  alone -- the inline twin-lookup in `group_protonation_variants`
  (`extract_fragment_smiles.py:80`, twin search ~102-108) scans the whole same-size bucket
  once per charge group, so plain keys are in the cost even though they never enter the
  branch. Charged fraction of the
  real dict is 7.4% over keys, 3.0% over representatives.
- Preprocessed DB has zero waters/monatomic ions (checked HOH/ZN/MG/CA/NA/CL/MN/FE across
  all 65,600 files). This corrupts protonation
  states everywhere and means metal-coordination vdGs can't exist in the current library.
  Raising trim radius does not fix this. Deferred to the next parent DB.
- Orphan-backbone-N (`_prep_filters.incomplete_backbone_resindices`, :22) and modified-residue
  handling (`_prep_filters.modified_residues_to_protein`, :314-395) are fixed in code and both
  applied by s01, but neither has been exercised on a fresh BioLiP input since the fix landed.
  Defer to the next refactor for parent DB.

AFTER THE 2026-09-07 VALIDATION RUN (library otherwise sound, zero invariant
violations)
- DECIDED FOR THIS BUILD 2026-09-21: mining checks every SMARTS edge against the
  provisional broad Cordero-radius envelope `0.70 <= d/(r_i+r_j) <= 1.25`, in
  addition to the 2.5 A component guard. Rejection is row-level, so sound observations
  of an affected fragment remain eligible and `stream_skipped_cg_bond_geometry` reports
  the exact rejected-row count. SMARTS bond class/order is retained in the screening
  input and diagnostics; ambiguous bonds use the same broad fallback.
- DEFERRED TO THE NEXT GEOMETRY REFACTOR: calibrate element-pair- and bond-class-specific
  bounds against trusted structures. The broad envelope is containment, not a validated
  bond-length model, and this production build explicitly accepts residual borderline
  contamination in order to salvage rows. A prior 40-fragment sample covered 86,790
  representative bond observations and rejected 7 of 22,898 rows, but the envelope also
  admitted known suspect short C-S/aromatic C-N and long C-O/C-C examples.

AFTER THIS REBUILD -- REQUIRED INPUT TO THE NEXT ONE
- Contact-gate threshold (`sasa.MIN_CONTACT_AREA`, ratified at 0.0) was chosen against
  divergence nulls at mirror scale. Once a library exists, re-decide it on geometric
  recurrence -- per contact-area band (0-0.25, 0.25-0.5, 0.5-1, 1-2, 2-5, >5 A^2), the
  histogram of `cluster_num_parents` (distinct PDB ENTRIES/depositions --
  see docs/directives/data_format.md; never `cluster_size`, which NCS
  inflates) and `cluster_pose_radius`. Tight clusters recurring across unrelated biounits
  = real interaction geometry; diffuse singletons = packing noise, so raise the threshold
  above that band next version. Cross-check the pi classes separately. Also re-run the
  CG-scoped sweep once the subgraph vocabulary exists: smaller fragments have less
  buriable surface, which makes any absolute threshold more binding. Full rationale:
  `private/project_planning/decision_records.md`, "DR-3 CLOSED".
- Cation-pi and S-pi to PROTEIN rings are a known limitation of the buried-SASA
  criterion, not a radius problem: 96% / 94% of the missed pairs are inside the 6.5 A
  prefilter with zero credited area, because the ligand-side moiety is a single atom
  whose sphere is mostly buried by its own molecule. Widening the prefilter buys ~4%.
  Decide whether these need a non-SASA term.

DESIGN DECISIONS (no urgent deadline)
- Protonation strategy: matching is currently charge-loose (library keys pool variants
  via `charge_normalized_fragment`/`group_protonation_variants`,
  `extract_fragment_smiles.py:68,80`); decide whether to make it charge-strict for
  donor/acceptor accuracy, or keep pooling and rely on the read-path warning
  (`hit_finder_core._warn_charge_only_miss:237`) for charged-key misses.
- Statistical-support denominator: NOW HAS DIRECT EVIDENCE, not just theory. In the
  2026-09-10 contact-gate analysis, apparent chemical enrichment across contact-area
  bands PASSED a burial/distance-matched null but FAILED a structure-level permutation
  null, i.e. the signal rode on shared ancestry (NCS copies, homologs, redeposits), and
  a multinomial null that treated those as independent draws was 2.7x too permissive.
  Any library-wide statistic compared against a null must permute at the parent-biounit
  level. See `private/project_planning/decision_records.md`, DR-3 23:1x entry.
- Statistical-support denominator: appears RESOLVED (verify before striking) --
  `identify_bioisosteres/common.py`'s `load_bucket_counts` (lines 89-129) and
  `hit_finder_core.py` (bucket read at line 616, threaded to output as
  `vdg_cluster_num_parents` at line 751, via `_BUCKET_ROW_FIELDS` at line 484) both now
  read `cluster_num_parents` directly rather than raw cluster/observation counts; it is
  written at `clus_and_deduplicate_vdgs.py:384`, not test-only.
- Nested fragments (`cccn` inside `cccnc`) share observations, so counts across fragments
  are correlated -- same issue as the overlapping-sites entry in pitfalls.md, but
  systematic once enumeration moves from balls to all connected subgraphs. Buckets are
  independent, so clustering is unaffected; normalize only when aggregating across
  fragments (bioisosteres, hit scoring).
- Calibrate Stage-1 RMSD cutoff `T` against recall on real mined environments (not stored
  nr vdGs). Blocks a real answer on the Stage-2 flanking-context question below.
- Stage 2 splits pose clusters by flanking context; unclear if that's a better use of
  representative budget than a tighter `T`. Needs the `T` calibration first.
- Query/library key reachability: build an explicit relaxation map (protonation, H-count)
  instead of the ad hoc `resolve_fragment_alias` + charge-only-miss warning. Ring-size
  differences should stay hard constraints (they change CG geometry) and already are
  (`Frags.ring_query_for_atom:152`).
- Decide how to treat icodes. De facto policy already in code: residues with >1 distinct
  icode are skipped on both sides (`dock_utils.py:47-49`,
  `align_and_cluster.py:761-762`; a lone icode is fine).
- `_has_deliberate_charge_assignment` (`utils.py:72`, called at `:109`): only fragment that
  would exercise it is below the selection threshold. Delete, or lower the threshold.

WHEN ADDING NEW LIGANDS / REBUILDING THE LIGAND FILE
- `UNDESIRED_ELEMENTS` (`fragment_database_ligs.py:217-222`) is a denylist and already
  leaks (12 CCD elements are neither organic nor denylisted); a carbon-bearing complex of
  one would be mined as an organic fragment. Switch to an allowlist
  (C,N,O,S,P,F,Cl,Br,I,B) before adding non-CCD ligands. Se is already decided -- it is
  denylisted (:219); B is deliberately NOT denylisted (boronic acids and boronate esters
  are real covalent warheads -- this rationale is not in a code comment, it's this doc's
  own record of the decision).
- Remove cryoprotectants in `fragment_database_ligs.py` -- no resname skip list exists
  there. The only nearby filter is much narrower and downstream:
  `extract_fragment_smiles.is_solvent_artifact:18` covers halogen oxyanions only.
- Hydrogens: no longer needed for contacts. But H is still
  load-bearing in one place: `ligand_perception.py:182` deletes them AFTER
  `PerceiveBondOrders`, which needs the positions. The CCD-template path doesn't care
  (H counts come from the template, :280-283), so the open question is narrower than it
  was: a new NON-CCD ligand has no template, lands on the OpenBabel fallback, and does
  still need H. Decide how to build them for that case.
- Consider a `keys_only` fast path in `fragment_database_ligs.py` that skips the
  permutation/site-grouping machinery (unneeded when only the element string is used) --
  verified no-op on CCD output, worth doing for a rebuild. Does not exist today.

OTHER / LOW PRIORITY
- Scrub irrelevant PDBs (PSI/PSII, ribosomes, heavy Fe-S cluster complexes, etc). The
  >62-chain skip in s01 incidentally catches some, but it is a chain-ID-encoding guard,
  not a content scrub.
- Add RSCC/RSR/RSRZ to per-vdG quality records. NOTE: the plumbing was REMOVED, not left
  as a no-op (`tests/test_vdg_miner_environments.py:140-142` asserts rscc/rsr/rsrz are
  not parameters of the mining entry point), so this means writing plumbing from scratch.
- Occupancy floor (`min_occ`, `external/vdG-miner/vdg_miner/vdg/vdg.py:239` default 0.3,
  applied `:360`) is currently inert (prepwizard flattens everything to 1.00). Satisfied
  as a standing note: keep it loose, don't filter on it even if a future DB preserves
  occupancy; measured values ride along per vdG (`clus_helpers.py:26`) so a stricter
  threshold can be applied downstream without re-mining.
- CG element-pinning check has a residual gap for unpinned (OR/negation) SMARTS atoms:
  `dock_utils.cg_element_symbols:157-177` returns None for any atom RDKit reports as
  atomic number 0, and callers must treat those slots as matching any element
  (`clus_and_deduplicate_vdgs.py:1488-1499`, `dock_utils.py:191-199`; `hit_finder_core.py`
  never calls `cg_element_symbols` -- only these two call sites exist repo-wide).
  Believed unreachable in production today -- that is a claim about the fragment dict,
  not something the code guarantees. Add a pitfalls.md entry if the dict ever gains such
  a key.
- [ ] check_library_after_build.py: add the remaining post-build invariants. Present:
      aliases-resolve, charged-forward, completeness, h-class-fields (delegates to
      `h_class_diagnostic --dry-run`). **[NEEDS USER INPUT]** the claim that
      `docs/database_generation_guide.md:334` mandates this after every build is false --
      the guide is 203 lines total and has no "dry-run"/"post-build mandate" language
      anywhere (only a plain `--out h_class.tsv` invocation at line 178). Either the
      mandate was never written into the guide or was removed; decide whether to add it
      there, and whether other post-build checks are actually expected/undocumented.
